INT. ROLLER RINK, ABANDONED - AFTERNOON

A cavernous room. Wood floor warped into waves. Mirror ball cracked and dull. Light slants through boarded windows.

DESMOND, sixteen, lanky, wearing his late father's denim jacket two sizes too big, sits on a bench lacing up scuffed skates. MIRELA, seventeen, shaved head, a silver ring in her eyebrow, leans against the rail holding a boombox. UNCLE FERENC, sixties, wiry, a flat cap, a cigarette unlit between his lips, stands at the far end by the dead organ.

Dust motes drift.

MIRELA
You said you could skate.

DESMOND
I said I used to.

He stands. Wobbles. She watches, arms crossed.

MIRELA
Used to when?

DESMOND
Six. With my dad.

She doesn't answer. He pushes off. His legs go sideways. He catches the rail, laughs, ashamed.

UNCLE FERENC
(calling out)
Knees, boy! Bend your knees!

DESMOND
I'm bending them!

UNCLE FERENC
You're bending your back!

Mirela sets the boombox down. Presses play. A scratchy waltz fills the rink. Desmond stops mid-wobble.

DESMOND
What is this?

MIRELA
Ferenc's. He ran this rink in the eighties.

UNCLE FERENC
Nineteen eighty-one to ninety-four. Best waltz night in Budapest-on-the-Hudson.

DESMOND
That's not a place.

UNCLE FERENC
It was a state of mind.

Ferenc takes off his cap. Straightens. For a second, he looks twenty years younger. He steps onto the floor in his street shoes and glides, light, impossible, a hand tucked behind his back.

Desmond stares. Mirela's mouth twitches toward a smile.

MIRELA
Told you.

DESMOND
Your uncle can dance.

MIRELA
He can do a lot of things.

UNCLE FERENC
(passing them)
Watch my feet, boy. Not my face.

Desmond watches. Tries. Pushes, glides, a foot slips, but he doesn't fall. He grins. Mirela whoops, the first honest sound she's made.

MIRELA
Again!

He pushes off a second time. Better. Ferenc spins back around, arms out, steadying him without touching.

UNCLE FERENC
That's it. Don't think. Your father didn't think.

DESMOND
You knew my dad?

UNCLE FERENC
Every Friday for six years. Worst skater I ever saw.

DESMOND
(small smile)
He'd have said the same about me.

UNCLE FERENC
He'd have lied. He loved watching you.

Desmond looks down. Mirela cranks the volume. The waltz swells in the empty hall.

MIRELA
Okay, Des. Stop crying and skate.

DESMOND
I'm not crying.

MIRELA
You're a little bit crying.

He wipes his face with a sleeve. Pushes forward. Ferenc takes his left, Mirela comes onto the floor and takes his right. The three of them make a slow, crooked circle under the dead mirror ball.
